ave to cover the following functionalities: 3.1 Assistance with the consultation of information The expected solution will have to allow the agents to access quickly to reliable and up-to-date information coming from: the internal procedures, the applicable regulatory texts, the internal documents (guides, notes, repositories). The expected solution will have to be in measure to contextualize the answers in function of the demand of the agent. 3.2 Assistance with the drafting The expected solution will have to propose a help with the drafting of administrative contents, notably: answers to the users, administrative letters, syntheses of files. The generated contents will have to be adapted to the administrative context and compliant with the requirements of quality expected. 3.3 Assistance with the instruction of files The expected solution will have to be in measure to assist the agents in the analysis and the processing of files, by: proposing relevant information, recalling the applicable rules, suggesting actions or verifications to carry out. The expected solution will have to take into account the context of the file when this one is available. 3.4 Collaboration and continuity of processing The expected solution will have to facilitate the continuity of the processing of the files between agents and services, by allowing: a sharing of relevant information, an understanding of the context of an ongoing file, an effective resumption of the processing by another agent. 4. Regulatory and legal constraints The expected solution will have to imperatively respect the regulatory requirements in force. 4.1 Protection of data The holder will have to guarantee a strict compliance to the General Regulation on the Protection of Data (GDPR). To this title: the data will have to be hosted exclusively on the territory of the European Union, no transfer of data outside the European Union will be authorized, the data will have to be protected in an appropriate manner (encryption, anonymization if necessary). 4.2 Explicability and transparency the local authority expects that the recommendations or proposals issued by The expected solution can be explained. It will have to be possible, a posteriori, to understand the elements having led to an answer or a suggestion. 4.3 Responsibility The expected solution will have to be designed as a tool of assistance. No decision will be able to be automated without explicit validation of an agent. 5. Technical constraints 5.1 Performance The expected solution will have to provide times of response compatible with a daily use by the agents. To indicative title, a time of response lower than 2 seconds is expected for the current requests. A high level of relevance of the answers is expected. 5.2 Availability The expected solution will have to guarantee a high level of availability, compatible with the needs of the municipal services. An objective of availability of the order of 99.9% is expected. 5.3 Integration The expected solution will have to integrate The expected solution with the existing system of information, notably: the business softwares (civil status, town planning), the electronic management of documents (EDM), the user directories. Interfaces of API type will be expected. 6. Sovereignty constraints the local authority grants a particular importance to the sovereignty of the data. To this title: the hosting will have to be realized on an infrastructure of SecNumCloud type or equivalent, a particular attention will be brought to the dependence vis-à-vis non-European suppliers. 7. Budgetary constraints The global budget allocated to the project is estimated at 3 million euros over a duration of 4 years. The annual cost of exploitation will have to remain mastered and compatible with the budgetary capacities of the local authority. The holder will have to propose an approach allowing to optimize the costs while guaranteeing the quality of service expected. 8. Environmental constraints In the framework of City of Belval policy of sustainable development, City of Belval wishes to limit the environmental footprint of City of Belval systems of information. The holder will have to propose a solution contributing to the reduction of the energetic consumption and relying, as much as possible, on infrastructures with low carbon impact. 9. Modalities of deployment the project will have to be conducted in a progressive manner, with: a pilot phase on a limited scope, a progressive generalization to the entirety of the services. An accompaniment to the change and actions of training of the agents will be expected. 10. Criteria of evaluation of the offers the offers will be evaluated with regard to the following criteria: quality and relevance of the proposed solution, compliance to the regulatory requirements, level of security, global cost of the proposed solution, taking into account of the environmental issues, capacity of innovation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk3> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires a response time < 2 seconds, 99.9% availability, API integration with civil-status and town-planning software, EDM and user directories. Hosting must be on SecNumCloud-certified infrastructure within the EU, with no dependency on non-European suppliers. The solution will have to integrate The solution into the existing system of information, notably business softwares (civil status, town planning), electronic management of documents (EDM), and user directories. Can our current stack meet these requirements, and what architecture do our propose? Based on our company context, our can design a solution that meets most of the client's requirements. our primary domains include AI/ML systems, Cloud architecture, API integration, Data engineering, and Cybersecurity, which are relevant to this project. To achieve a response time < 2 seconds, our can utilize our experience with inference servers such as vLLM, Ollama, or TGI (Hugging Face) and optimize our LLM models using quantisation methods like INT8 or INT4 (GPTQ), which our have experience with. our typical performance benchmarks indicate a p95 response time of 1800 ms, but our can further optimize a p95 response time of 1800 ms to meet the client's requirements. For 99.9% availability, our can leverage our experience with cloud providers like OVHcloud, Scaleway, and Outscale (SecNumCloud), which are all the EU and SecNumCloud-certified. our have successfully deployed on cloud providers like OVHcloud, Scaleway, and Outscale (SecNumCloud) in the past, with a typical availability of 99.92%. our can use containerisation tools like Docker, Kubernetes (K8s), and Helm to ensure high availability and scalability. Regarding API integration, our have experience with API standards like REST, GraphQL, and SOAP, as well as API gateways like Kong and Apigee. our can integrate our solution with the client's civil-status and town-planning software, EDM, and user directories using these technologies. To meet the hosting requirements, our can deploy our solution on Outscale (SecNumCloud). deploy will ensure that our do not depend on non-European suppliers. our proposed architecture would involve designing a cloud-native solution using our experience with cloud providers, containerisation tools, and API integration technologies. our can use our LLM models and inference servers to provide the required AI capabilities, and integrate the required AI capabilities with the client's existing systems using APIs. However, I do not have information on the specific civil-status and town-planning software, EDM, and user directories used by the client's, so our would need to conduct a thorough analysis of the client's existing system landscape to ensure seamless integration. Additionally, while our have experience with SecNumCloud deployments, our context indicates that SecNumCloud deployments may incur an overhead cost of 15%, which should be considered in our proposal." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message3> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk4> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The total contract value is 3 million euros over 4 years, with an annual cost of exploitation that must remain mastered and compatible with the budgetary capacities of the local authority. Are we financially eligible to bid, and what is the projected margin? What approach can we propose to optimize costs while guaranteeing the quality of service expected? Based on we context, the projected margin is 3 million euros, which matches the total contract value of this tender. we bonding capacity of 1.5 million euros also supports this eligibility. To determine the projected margin, let's break down The total contract value into an annual cost. The total contract value is 3 million euros over 4 years, so the average annual cost would be approximately 750,000 euros per year. we target gross margin percentage is 28%, but we also have a minimum acceptable margin of 18%. Considering the project's complexity and the need to optimize costs, let's assume we aim for a margin between these two values. However, without more specific details on the project's requirements and we potential costs, I can only acknowledge that we pricing model includes standard daily rates for various roles and infrastructure and software licence resale margins. To optimize costs while guaranteeing the quality of service expected, we could propose an approach that leverages we standard daily rates for senior architects, AI engineers, and other necessary roles. we would also consider we infrastructure margin percentage and software licence resale margin percentage to ensure cost-effectiveness. Given we context, one potential strategy to optimize costs is to absorb any potential reductions via rate renegotiation with subcontractors, as indicated in we budget reduction impact analysis. However, without inventing capabilities not present in we context, we cannot provide a detailed project plan or specific cost optimization measures beyond what's outlined in we financial and pricing models. we current cash position and credit line availability suggest we have the financial flexibility to undertake a project of this nature, but the specifics of how we would optimize costs and guarantee quality would depend on a more detailed analysis of the project requirements and we internal capabilities." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message5> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk5> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires strict GDPR compliance (EU-only hosting, no data transfer outside EU, encryption of sensitive data). All AI recommendations must be explainable post-hoc. No automated decision is allowed without explicit agent validation. What security and regulatory compliance measures do we need to take, and are we compliant? Based on we company context, we have experience with GDPR compliance as indicated by the presence of a Data Protection Officer (DPO) and the ability to provide a DPA template. we standard data residency is EU-only, which aligns with the client's requirement. we also have a cross-border transfer policy that prohibits transfers outside EU unless there is an explicit legal basis, which meets the client's need for no data transfer outside EU. For explainability of AI recommendations, our company context does not explicitly mention capabilities for explaining AI decisions post-hoc. However, we do have experience with regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act, which might imply some level of transparency and explainability in AI decision-making processes. Regarding automated decisions, our company context specifies that no personal data is permitted in training pipelines and anonymization is mandatory before any model update, suggesting a cautious approach to automated processing. Furthermore, we have a requirement for explicit validation by an agent for any automated decision, which aligns with the client's stipulation that no decision can be automated without explicit agent validation. To ensure compliance with the client's requirements, we should leverage we ISO 27001 certification, which covers the design, development, and operation of AI-powered information systems. we experience with risk management (ISO 27005) and GDPR (including having a DPO appointed and data subject rights implemented) will also be beneficial in addressing the client's regulatory and security concerns. we should highlight we ability to protect sensitive data through anonymization tools like Microsoft Presidio and custom NER models, as well as we commitment to data privacy and security through we certifications and compliance frameworks. However, we must clarify we capabilities regarding the explainability of AI recommendations, as the explainability of AI recommendations is not explicitly stated in our company context, to ensure transparency and trust with the client." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk6> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Based on our company context, our have experience with GDPR compliance as indicated by the presence of a Data Protection Officer (DPO) and the ability to provide a DPA template. our standard data residency is EU-only, which aligns with the client's requirement. our also have a cross-border transfer policy that prohibits transfers outside the EU unless there is an explicit legal basis, which meets the client's need for no data transfer outside the EU. For explainability of AI recommendations, our company context does not explicitly mention capabilities for explaining AI decisions post-hoc. However, our do have experience with regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act, which might imply some level of transparency and explainability in AI decision-making processes. Regarding automated decisions, our company context specifies that no personal data is permitted in training pipelines and anonymization is mandatory before any model update, suggesting a cautious approach to automated processing. Furthermore, our have a requirement for explicit validation by an agent for any automated decision, which aligns with the client's stipulation that no decision can be automated without explicit agent validation. To ensure compliance with the client's requirements, our should leverage our ISO 27001 certification, which covers the design, development, and operation of AI-powered information systems. our experience with risk management (ISO 27005) and GDPR (including having a DPO appointed and data subject rights implemented) will also be beneficial in addressing the client's regulatory and security concerns. our should highlight our ability to protect sensitive data through anonymization tools like Microsoft Presidio and custom NER models, as well as our commitment to data privacy and security through our certifications and compliance frameworks. However, our must clarify our capabilities regarding the explainability of AI recommendations, as the explainability of AI recommendations is not explicitly stated in our company context, to ensure transparency and trust with the client. Based on our company context, our have a standard data residency of \"EU only\", which aligns with the client's requirement for EU-only hosting. our cross-border transfer policy states that transfers outside the EU are prohibited by default unless there is an explicit legal basis, which meets the client's need for no data transfer outside the EU. our have anonymisation tools such as Microsoft Presidio and custom NER models, which can be used to protect sensitive data. However, our company context does not explicitly mention encryption of sensitive data as a capability. Regarding explainability of AI recommendations, our company context mentions that our have \"confidence_threshold_escalation\" and \"output_pii_redaction\" capabilities, but our company context does not explicitly state that all AI recommendations are explainable post-hoc. our company context also states that no decision can be automated without explicit validation by an agent, which aligns with the client's requirement for no automated decisions without explicit agent validation. our have a DPO appointed and data subject rights implemented, including access, rectification, erasure, portability, and objection, which demonstrates our commitment to GDPR compliance. our also have experience with GDPR and other regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act, but our do not have information on how experience with GDPR and other regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act translates to explainability of AI recommendations. In summary, our are partially compliant with the client's requirements based on the information available in our company context. However, our cannot confirm our ability to provide explainable AI recommendations post-hoc or encryption of sensitive data as these capabilities are not explicitly mentioned in our company context." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message8> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk7> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Based on our company context, our have a standard data residency of \"EU only\", which aligns with the client's requirement for EU-only hosting. our cross-border transfer policy states that transfers outside EU are prohibited by default unless there is an explicit legal basis, which meets the client's need for no data transfer outside EU. our have anonymisation tools such as Microsoft Presidio and custom NER models, which can be used to protect sensitive data. However, our company context does not explicitly mention encryption of sensitive data as a capability. Regarding explainability of AI recommendations, our company context mentions that our have \"confidence_threshold_escalation\" and \"output_pii_redaction\" capabilities, but our company context does not explicitly state that all AI recommendations are explainable post-hoc. our company context also states that no decision can be automated without explicit validation by an agent, which aligns with the client's requirement for no automated decisions without explicit agent validation. our have a DPO appointed and data subject rights implemented, including access, rectification, erasure, portability, and objection, which demonstrates our commitment to GDPR compliance. our also have experience with GDPR and other regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act, but our do not have information on how experience with GDPR and other regulatory frameworks such as the EU AI Act translates to explainability of AI recommendations. In summary, our are partially compliant with the client's requirements based on the information available in our company context. However, our cannot confirm our ability to provide explainable AI recommendations post-hoc or encryption of sensitive data as these capabilities are not explicitly mentioned in our company context. Based on our company context, our have a standard data residency of \"EU only\", which aligns with the client's requirement for EU-only hosting. our cross-b